EDITOR HELP
===========

A vi-like text editor for the 6502 platform.

Usage: editor <filename>

If the file does not exist, starts with an empty buffer.
If no filename is given, starts with [No Name].
Maximum file size: ~40KB. Files exceeding this are truncated and
opened read-only.
Maximum lines: 1023. Longer files are truncated and opened read-only,
and an edit that would add a 1024th line is refused with "Buffer full".


MODES
-----

The editor has three modes:

  NORMAL   Default mode for navigation and commands.
  INSERT   Text entry mode. Entered via i, a, A, o, O, s, S, C, cc, cw, cb, ce.
  COMMAND  Colon command-line mode. Entered via :.


NORMAL MODE - MOVEMENT
----------------------

  h, Left        Move cursor left (accepts count)
  l, Right       Move cursor right (accepts count)
  j, Down        Move cursor down (accepts count)
  k, Up          Move cursor up (accepts count)
  0, Home        Move to beginning of line
  $, End         Move to end of line (last character)
  ^              Move to first non-blank character on line
  w              Move to start of next word (accepts count)
  b              Move to start of previous word (accepts count)
  e              Move to end of current/next word (accepts count)
  Ctrl-Right     Move to start of next word (same as w)
  Ctrl-Left      Move to start of previous word (same as b)
  G              Go to last line; [N]G goes to line N
  gg             Go to first line
  Ctrl-F, PgDn   Scroll forward one screen (accepts count)
  Ctrl-B, PgUp   Scroll backward one screen (accepts count)
  Ctrl-D         Scroll forward half a screen (accepts count)
  Ctrl-U         Scroll backward half a screen (accepts count)

  Word motions cross line boundaries. Words are classified as:
  word characters (a-z, A-Z, 0-9, _), punctuation, or whitespace.
  A word boundary is a transition between classes.

  Ctrl-D and Ctrl-U accept a count that becomes "sticky" - the
  same scroll amount is reused on subsequent Ctrl-D/Ctrl-U presses
  until a new count is given. Without a count, they scroll half a
  screen by default.

  G, gg, Ctrl-F, Ctrl-B, Ctrl-D, Ctrl-U, 'a and :N put the cursor on
  the first non-blank character of the line they go to, as dd and :d
  do on the line after the deleted ones.

  j, k, Up and Down (in insert mode too) remember the column they
  started from: a shorter line on the way puts the cursor at its end,
  and the next longer line takes it back to that column. After $ or
  End they keep to the end of each line.


NORMAL MODE - EDITING
---------------------

All editing commands are blocked in read-only mode.

  i              Enter insert mode at cursor
  a              Enter insert mode after cursor
  A              Enter insert mode at end of line
  o              Open new line below, enter insert mode
  O              Open new line above, enter insert mode
  x, Delete      Delete character at cursor (accepts count)
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  X              Delete character before cursor (accepts count)
                 Stops at column 0; yanks deleted characters
  D              Delete from cursor to end of line
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  d$             Delete from cursor to end of line (accepts count)
                 With count N, deletes through end of line N-1 below
  d0             Delete from beginning of line to cursor
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  dd             Delete current line (accepts count: [N]dd)
                 Yanks deleted lines first (line yank)
  dw             Delete from cursor to next word boundary (accepts count)
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  db             Delete backward to word start (accepts count)
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  de             Delete from cursor to end of word (accepts count)
                 Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  cc             Delete current line, open blank line, enter insert mode
                 (accepts count: [N]cc). Yanks deleted lines (line yank)
  cw             Change word: delete to end of word, enter insert mode
                 (accepts count). Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  cb             Change backward: delete to word start, enter insert mode
                 (accepts count). Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  ce             Change to end of word: delete to end of word, enter
                 insert mode (accepts count). Yanks deleted characters
  C              Delete from cursor to end of line, enter insert mode
                 (accepts count). Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  s              Delete character(s) at cursor, enter insert mode
                 (accepts count). Yanks deleted characters (character yank)
  S              Delete current line, open blank line, enter insert mode
                 Same as cc (alias)
  r              Replace character at cursor (accepts count: [N]r)
                 Waits for next keypress, replaces N characters
                 (a printable key or Tab). Enter or Ctrl-J replaces the
                 N characters with one line break, splitting the line,
                 and does nothing if fewer than N are left. Any other
                 key cancels
  ~              Toggle case of character at cursor (accepts count)
                 Advances cursor after each toggle
  J              Join current line with next line (accepts count: [N]J)
                 Replaces newline with a space. With count N, joins N lines
                 (1J joins two). Up to 129 lines join at a time: more is
                 refused with "Too many lines to join"
  >>             Indent current line by 2 spaces (accepts count: [N]>>)
  <<             Unindent current line by up to 2 spaces (accepts count)
                 Both leave the cursor on the first non-blank character
  yy             Yank (copy) current line (accepts count: [N]yy)
                 Does not modify the file
  yw             Yank from cursor to next word boundary (accepts count)
                 Character yank. Does not modify the file
  yb             Yank backward to word start (accepts count)
                 Character yank. Cursor moves to start of yanked region
  ye             Yank from cursor to end of word (accepts count)
                 Character yank. Does not modify the file
  y$             Yank from cursor to end of line (accepts count)
                 Character yank. Does not modify the file
  y0             Yank from beginning of line to cursor
                 Character yank. Cursor does not move
  p              Paste after/below cursor (accepts count: [N]p)
                 Line yank: pastes lines below current line
                 Char yank: inserts after cursor position
  P              Paste before/above cursor (accepts count: [N]P)
                 Line yank: pastes lines above current line
                 Char yank: inserts before cursor position
                 A line paste leaves the cursor on the first non-blank
                 character of the first line pasted
  u              Undo last edit / redo (toggles between undo and redo)
                 Single-level: undoes the most recent operation, then
                 pressing u again redoes it. A new edit replaces the
                 undo history. Supports undo of: dd, x, X, D, dw, db, de,
                 d$, d0, J, p, P, r, ~, >>, <<, :[range]d, :[range]>
                 and :[range]<.
                 o, O, cw, cb, ce, cc, s, C and S can be undone only when
                 ESC follows with no text typed: text typed in insert mode
                 is not undoable and clears the undo history.
                 A yank (yy, yw, y$, :y, ...) ends the undo of a delete
                 or paste, as their undo restores from the yank buffer.
                 Rapidly typed (batched) keys execute together but undo
                 as if processed one at a time: u undoes the last one.


NORMAL MODE - SEARCH
--------------------

  /              Enter forward search prompt. Type a pattern and press
                 Enter. Empty pattern reuses the previous search.
                 ESC cancels. Search is literal (no regex).
  ?              Enter backward search prompt. Same behavior as /
                 but searches backward through the file.
  n              Repeat last search in the same direction (wraps around)
  N              Repeat last search in the opposite direction (wraps around)

  If the pattern is not found, "Pattern not found" is displayed.
  A pattern can be as long as fits the status row after the prompt
  (the screen width less 2 characters, at most 127); further keys
  are ignored. ESC, or backspacing past the prompt, cancels the
  search and keeps the previous pattern for n and N.

  As in vi, the matches on a line do not overlap: each search goes
  on from the end of the match before, so in "abababab" /abab and n
  find the matches at columns 1 and 5, not 3.


NORMAL MODE - MARKS
-------------------

  ma             Set mark 'a' at current line (a-z available)
  'a             Jump to mark 'a' (cursor goes to the first
                 non-blank character)

  Marks are automatically adjusted when lines are inserted or
  deleted. Marks within a deleted range are cleared (this includes
  lines replaced with cc or S).


NORMAL MODE - COUNT PREFIX
--------------------------

  Type a number (1-9 to start, then 0-9) before a command to
  repeat it. Examples:

    5j             Move down 5 lines
    3dd            Delete 3 lines
    10x            Delete 10 characters
    2p             Paste 2 times
    4w             Move forward 4 words
    3>>            Indent 3 lines
    5~             Toggle case of 5 characters
    3J             Join 3 lines (current + 2 below)
    2dw            Delete 2 words forward
    3de            Delete to end of 3rd word

  Digits typed once a count has reached 1000 are ignored, so the
  largest count is 9999. h, l, j, k, w, b, e, x, X, ~, r, J, Ctrl-F,
  Ctrl-B and the word operators (dw, cw, yw, ...) take a count of at
  most 255. ESC clears an active count.
  0 alone moves to line start; it does not start a count.


NORMAL MODE - OTHER
-------------------

  :              Enter command-line mode
  ESC            Clear count prefix and/or pending two-key command


INSERT MODE
-----------

  Printable chars   Insert at cursor position
  Tab               Insert tab character
  Enter             Split line (insert newline)
  Backspace         Delete character before cursor; at column 0,
                    joins with the previous line
  Delete            Delete character at cursor; at end of line,
                    joins with the next line
  ESC               Return to normal mode (cursor moves back one).
                    A lone ESC takes effect after 0.1 s: the editor
                    waits that long for the rest of an arrow or other
                    special key, which on a slow link comes later

  When the text buffer is full, a char or Enter that does not fit
  is refused with "Buffer full", and the next key dismisses the
  message. Keys typed ahead go in exactly as if typed one at a time.

  Arrow keys, Home, End, PgUp, PgDn, Ctrl-F, Ctrl-B all work
  for navigation within insert mode. Ctrl-Right and Ctrl-Left
  move by word. The cursor can be positioned one past the last
  character (for appending).


COMMAND-LINE MODE
-----------------

  :w             Save file
  :q             Quit (warns if file is modified)
  :wq            Save and quit
  :q!            Quit without saving
  :[N]           Go to line N (1-based)
  :marks         Display all set marks with line numbers, a
                 screen at a time: at "-- More --" a key shows
                 the next page, q ends the list

  Range commands:

  :[start],[end]d    Delete lines in range (yanks first)
  :[start],[end]y    Yank (copy) lines in range
  :[start],[end]>    Indent lines in range by 2 spaces
  :[start],[end]<    Unindent lines in range by up to 2 spaces
                     (the cursor goes to the first non-blank character
                     of the range's first line)

  Range positions can be:
    N              Line number (1-based)
    'a             Mark reference
    .              Current line
  An omitted position is the current line (:5,d = :5,.d and
  :,5d = :.,5d).

  A single position can be used instead of a range:
    :[pos]d            Delete one line at position
    :[pos]>            Indent one line at position

  Bare :> and :< apply to the current line.

  Examples:
    :1,5d          Delete lines 1 through 5
    :'a,'bd        Delete from mark a to mark b
    :.,'ay         Yank from current line to mark a
    :3,3d          Delete just line 3
    :1,10>         Indent lines 1 through 10
    :'a,.<         Unindent from mark a to current line
    :>             Indent current line
    :5>            Indent line 5

  If start > end, the range is automatically swapped. Line numbers
  past the end of the file mean the last line. A range with no
  command goes to its last line (:1,5 = :5).

  The report of :w ("name" written) or of a range command (N lines
  deleted, yanked, shifted) stays on the status row until the next
  key.

  ESC cancels command input. Backspace edits the command;
  backspacing past the start cancels. A command can be as long as
  fits the status row after the ':' (the screen width less 2
  characters, at most 127); further keys are ignored.


YANK BUFFER
-----------

  The yank buffer (4KB) holds either complete lines or character
  data, depending on the last yank/delete operation:

  Line yank (from dd, yy, cc, range commands):
    p/P paste complete lines above or below.

  Character yank (from x, D, d$, d0, dw, db, de, yw, yb, ye,
  y$, y0, cw, cb, ce, s, C):
    p inserts after cursor (or at line start if empty line).
    P inserts before cursor.

  Each new yank/delete clears the previous yank buffer contents.
  A yank, delete or change of more than 4KB is refused with
  "Yank buffer full": the text, the yank buffer and undo stay
  as they were.


STATUS BAR
----------

  The bottom line shows:
    filename  [RO]  [+]  mode  count/pending  line,col  total

  [RO]  = read-only (file was truncated: too large or too many lines)
  [+]   = file has been modified since last save

  The status bar and messages are cut off one column short of the
  screen width.


DISPLAY
-------

  Long lines wrap across multiple screen rows, up to 255 rows per
  line (255 times the screen width: 10200 characters at 40 columns,
  20400 at 80). Text past a line's 255th row is kept and saved, but
  it is not displayed correctly: it can show out of place, and with
  the cursor there the view and the cursor's place on screen are
  wrong.
  Lines past end of file are shown as ~ (tilde).
  Non-ASCII bytes (high bit set) are shown as ? in reverse video.
  Tab characters are shown as > in reverse video.
  Other control characters are shown as ? in reverse video.
